@page "/projects/portfolio/stack"
@page "/projects/web-development/frontend"

<div class="project-page page-content">
	<header class="project-header">
		<p class="eyebrow">Web Development</p>
		<h1>Frontend</h1>
		<p class="tagline">Blazor-first presentation with a terminal-inspired layout and focused, readable project pages.</p>
	</header>

	<section class="project-section">
		<h2>Portfolio UI</h2>
		<p>
			The portfolio is built with Razor components and Blazor Server. The main shell uses a persistent header, a tree-style side navigation, and content pages that are intentionally constrained for comfortable reading.
		</p>
		<p>
			Recent updates include a Links page with accessible text labels, Font Awesome brand icons, external profile links, and a responsive grid that keeps the content usable on narrow screens.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="project-section">
		<h2>Professional Frontend Background</h2>
		<div class="project-grid">
			<article>
				<h3>Blazor</h3>
				<p>Built full-stack solutions with C#, ASP.NET Core, and Blazor, including production-oriented UI work and internal tools.</p>
			</article>
			<article>
				<h3>Angular</h3>
				<p>Developed Angular-based UIs backed by C# and .NET APIs across customer-facing and time-sensitive environments.</p>
			</article>
			<article>
				<h3>Responsive CSS</h3>
				<p>Uses scoped component styles, responsive grids, stable spacing, and readable typography across portfolio sections.</p>
			</article>
			<article>
				<h3>Unity UI</h3>
				<p>Published an iOS application using Unity and C#, including gameplay UI, platform compliance, and release ownership.</p>
			</article>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="project-section">
		<h2>Frontend Priorities</h2>
		<ul class="project-list">
			<li>Keep the first screen useful instead of turning the portfolio into a marketing splash page.</li>
			<li>Make navigation predictable so visitors can move quickly between resume, links, and project pages.</li>
			<li>Use real text labels with icons so links stay accessible and understandable.</li>
			<li>Prefer maintainable component styling over one-off visual tricks.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>
</div>
